00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.470 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  many were amazed others shocked when  elisa longkong an indigenous mapuche  woman was chosen to lead chile's  constitutional convention  two years ago it would have been  unthinkable  but despite or perhaps because of an  increasingly uncontrollable land  conflict in south central chile  recognition of indigenous chileans who  make up 12 percent of this country took  center stage in the writing of the new  magna carta  the new constitution begins by stating  that chile is a social democratic  law-based employee national state  chile has always been pluri national  except no constitution before has ever  recognised the existence of indigenous  people  while there are scores of countries that  recognize the culture and language of  their indigenous nations with varying  degrees of autonomy chile's new  constitution if approved would go  further  the constitution includes the  restitution of ancestral land and what  we should see in many years through  approval of various laws is much more  land returned and distributed to  indigenous communities  [Music]  until 150 years ago the lion's share of  chile belonged to the mapuche today the  poorest sector society  but the concept of indigenous autonomy  recognition of indigenous laws and most  of all returning land that in many cases  is now occupied by non-indigenous  chilean families and industries  horrifies many they said a new one  former social development minister  alfredo moreno says it will cause more  conflict which nobody knows  what is that land because it used to  have doesn't have a meaning on time  what the land that they have 200 years  ago 100 years ago yesterday today then  it says that  that territory  they can have the their own government  they can have  the  they could be autonomous from the rest  of the nation  chile would be only the third country  after ecuador and bolivia to define  itself as pluri national  but that does not mean as some believe  that chile would be split up into 11  independent indigenous states  we have never proposed to install a  different state it must be understood  that the chilean state will not be  divided with different laws everything  must be within the framework of the same  constitution and the same state  the constitution would allow indigenous  communities to veto or profit from  investments on their land in chile's  lithium and copper rich north  or the forestry ridge south for example  as always the key is in the details  the laws to implement the new prairie  national state have not yet been written  while armed mapuche rebel groups dismiss  the whole exercise as a farce  many others are counting on a new  constitution to address historical  wrongs and in the process start an era  of peaceful coexistence and equality for  all  lucia newman al jazeera santiago chile

